this one road it's time  the story of Marilyn Monroe has been  told time and time again but blonde  definitely stands out from the crowd  from Anna De armas's incredible grounded  depiction of the star the unique way it  was filmed to the way they asked Marilyn  herself for permission to bring her back  to life  number one  stepping into the shoes of Marilyn  Monroe is no easy feat the cast and crew  of this movie got this big card and  wrote a message to the late Norma Jean  basically asking for her permission to  make the film and left the card on her  grave Anna De armas didn't take  Marilyn's position lightly she claims  they all felt her presence on set I  truly believe that she was very close to  us she was with us I think she was happy  she would also throw things off the wall  sometimes and get mad if she didn't like  something maybe this sounds very  mystical but it is true we all felt it  number two it took nine months to  perfect it just because it's so iconic  and so specific and English is not my  first language Anna did not shy away  from the hard work this film required  director Andrew Dominic had been  preparing for it for over 10 years he  actually asked me how much time do you  think you need and I was like well  forever she had a lot to catch up on she  worked with dialect coaches every every  day for several hours watched her films  and was always on YouTube watching  anything Marilyn Monroe she could find  number three  this might not seem like a big deal to  some but I just didn't leave home for  two months though painful for her to go  through it was necessary to really sell  the character they even took her to an  eyebrow specialist to get them  professionally done to mimic Marilyn's  brows not only that I like shaved the  middle of my eyebrows to make them more  separate them like she had the reason  why she didn't leave her house though  was because her hair was still brunette  so the whole thing just looked  incredibly awkward it was terrifying  number four there were five of them Ana  de armas was never going to color her  hair for the role of Marilyn not even  because she didn't want to but because  her hair wasn't the right texture Rob  Pickens the LA based wig maker and his  team spent over 150 hours hand making  each one when I say detail down to the  knot Maryland's Corners grew  horizontally so we tied those hairs  horizontally we looked like crazy people  sometimes with photos pinned all over  the wall all while we're tying  number five Ana de armas's eyes were  actually the one thing that made her  look similar to Marilyn Monroe Anna has  hazel eyes so she had to wear blue  contacts while filming she also had to  wear false lashes but only to weigh her  eyes down Tina Rossler Kerwin head of  the makeup Department discusses her  thought process behind the decision I  used a lot of lashes to weigh the  corners of dearmus's eyes down because  her eyes are a little bit bigger but the  lashes if you put enough of them on  it'll change the shape of your eye  number six to look that much like  Marilyn definitely took some time in the  makeup chair that was quite a journey  every day it was about three hours to  get ready every morning they relied  heavily on their Incredible Crew to  bring Marilyn to life we have over more  than 100 wardrobe changes in the movie  and they're all so beautiful and iconic  it was a challenge every day because the  film covers 36 years of Maryland's life  the crew had to effectively show that  time lapse in her costume hair and make  every day when we achieve that look and  we looked in the mirror and we knew we  got it was like really beautiful number  seven the cameras were always rolling  onadamus is not an actor who likes to  stay in character while the cameras are  off Andrew wanted to capture something  more spontaneous but the way Andrew  Dominic filmed the movie made that a  little difficult he had this thing that  he actually never caught  in between takes he even had his entire  crew dressed in clothes appropriate for  the time he would have that freedom of  let the camera follow me around set  there were a lot of improvised moments  captured on set as well as whenever she  would get her hair or makeup fixed or  get a note from her dialect coach if  there was someone from the crew in the  shot then it would be okay they were  dressed in in Period clothes number  eight  Anna De armas watched a ton of Marilyn  Monroe movies when she was behind the  camera Andrew Dominic had two monitors  pulled up one of her and one of the real  Marilyn in the scene she was recreating  they wanted every angle to be perfect so  of course that shot a ton of takes so on  a day armas has clocked hundreds of  hours of Marilyn Monroe movies under her  belt also being in every frame of the  entire movie Anna really felt the strain  of Marilyn Monroe's heavy character  it was exhausting I just cannot imagine  what it was like to be her number nine  you wouldn't be able to tell but this  movie was actually a rush job it was  done in just nine weeks which is a  ridiculously short amount of time to  film a movie that clocks in at almost  three hours we didn't have money we  didn't have time to do this to do the  movie it didn't help that they faced  several delays due to covid-19 and had  to push the release date back number 10.  if you know the story of Marilyn Monroe  you know it is very rarely a happy one  on a day armis felt this heaviness for  the entirety of the nine weeks she spent  working on the film she couldn't shake  it though she didn't go method and stay  in character when the cameras were off  her emotional well-being still took a  beating there was no way to unplug  because I'd get home and study for the  next day and then Andrew was on the  phone until midnight I would go to sleep  and dream I had long conversations with  her some days I would go home and have  dinner and as I was washing the dishes I  would just start sobbing crying and  crying because I had this terrible  feeling I knew I couldn't fix it Marilyn  Monroe only exists on the screen number  11. speaking of getting into character  let's talk about the first time Anna  looked in the mirror and saw Marilyn  Monroe everyone in the room started  crying I can tell you that it was it was  very emotional it was definitely a much  more surreal experience than the  Maryland costume she wore during her  audition I was just like driving down  Hollywood Boulevard with this very cheap  blonde wig and some kind of like makeup  but her first day on set everyone was  completely overwhelmed by how much like  Maryland she looked felt like  she was back or something I don't know  number 12 with great power comes great  responsibility anode armas admitted she  went through a lot of fear and  insecurity when playing this role most  of the time I thought I was doing it  wrong I was thinking what are these  American actors thinking of me they know  this person better than me they've grown  up with her what are they thinking about  my accent Andrew Dominic thought those  emotions were perfect that's it go with  it because that is literally what she's  going through as she could channel that  into her performance that is exactly  what she's feeling number 13. also known  as Mafia a fun fact about this film is  Anna De armas's 13 year old Maltese  Elvis is in a scene when I watch it I  don't know how to feel because of course  it's a very serious important scene in  the film and then at the same time I'm  just looking at him and I'm melting he  starred in two scenes but one of them  ended up being cut this isn't the first  time he's been on a film set apparently  he's so well behaved she's able to take  him to work quite often number 14.  Andrew Dominic had to get this movie  made blonde would never let me go for  years he would continue to think about  the movie and get new ideas for it even  Lynn Blum fell apart I'd still be  thinking about blondes after 10 years of  trying to make it happen he pushed  through all the delays lack of funding  and even the lack of time and finally  brought it to the big screen number 15.  Andrew Dominic isn't holding anything  back he thinks about what it would have  been like had the movie been released a  few years ago if it had come out a few  years ago it would have come out right  when me too hit and it would have been  an expression of all that stuff he  thinks that because it's being released  now it's definitely going to offend  everyone who watches it it's a film that  definitely has a morality about it but  it swims in very ambiguous Waters  because I don't think it will be as cut  and dry as people want to see it there's  something in it to offend everyone let  us know in the comments we you think  about the movie  blonde is a powerful fictional biopic  brought to life by the incredible  performance of Anna De armas the  passionate direction of Andrew Dominic  and a crew who can make miracles happen  even under the stunting pressure of time  what were you most surprised to find out  about the making of This film

